# Line endings.
#
# Development happens on Windows; deployment happens on Linux. Git's autocrlf
# would hand these files to the build host with CRLF, and a few of them break
# on a trailing carriage return rather than ignoring it:
#
#   Procfile          -- the \r lands inside the port argument, so uvicorn is
#                        asked to bind "$PORT\r" and the process never starts
#   runtime.txt       -- the version string stops matching a known runtime
#   .python-version   -- same
#   *.sh              -- /bin/sh reports "bad interpreter: no such file"
#
# Forcing LF on them costs nothing on Windows: no editor here cares, and
# nothing in this project reads them locally.

* text=auto

Procfile        text eol=lf
runtime.txt     text eol=lf
.python-version text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never tries to translate anything inside them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.jks binary
*.keystore binary
